    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper studies the organization and decision-making process of the Ministry of State Security’s (MSS) regarding cyber-operations through three hypotheses. The first hypothesis explores the MSS's centralized goal-setting mechanism. It examines the MSS’ structure from the State Council down to regional bureaus, focusing on intelligence collection pipelines. It also evaluates how national priorities and intelligence requirements are shaped, utilizing broad and vague terminologies rooted in traditional slogan politics, and how cyber-operations at the APT level align with strategic plans such as the 14th Five Year Plan and Made in China 2025. The second hypothesis challenges the first by suggesting a decentralized execution of cyber-operations within the MSS, through the varied command relationships among hacker groups, front companies, and MSS authorities under the contractor model. By studying the operational autonomy of groups like APT41 and the control dynamics in cases like APT40, the paper assesses the decentralization in cyber operations and explores the nature of relationships—whether competitive or coordinated—among different hacking groups. The third hypothesis argues that the MSS has priority access to zero-day vulnerabilities and influences their disclosure, thereby gaining strategic advantages in cyber-operations. This hypothesis is examined through the lens of the 2021 Regulations on the Management of Network Product Security Vulnerabilities, which has facilitated the MSS's capability to exploit these vulnerabilities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Saade, T. (2024). China’s Ministry of State Security (MSS): Organization and Processes of Cyber Decision Making. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/mn464cd2510. https://doi.org/10.25740/mn464cd2510.",
